\section*{Linear rigidity of bi-Lipschitz equivalent separable Banach spaces}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 324; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that a global bi-Lipschitz bijection can exist between separable real Banach spaces for which no linear isomorphism exists.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 324; 2026-09-24), weighted graph spaces give a linear obstruction detected by complete continuity. A triangular change of variables is localized into moving Hilbert slots with common motion budgets and orthogonality; finite inverses converge to an onto nonlinear equivalence.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The displayed question has a negative answer under the manuscript's stated construction. Its counterexample meets the conditions in this problem and defeats the universal assertion, so this is a full disproof claim. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 324; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 324; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: a global bi-Lipschitz bijection can exist between separable real Banach spaces for which no linear isomorphism exists.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{C0Absorption.lean}, \texttt{LipschitzEquivalence.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.C0Absorption.main\_result}, \texttt{OAI.LipschitzCounterexample.main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

